  "recommendation": "Do not adopt a single unified 'artifact distribution' abstraction covering napi/maturin/CLI-binaries/Homebrew in one spec. The evidence splits into three tiers that warrant different treatment, and forcing one model now would either over-scope the near-term work or under-scope the later work.\n\nTier 1 — ship now, smallest lift, already designed: close the napi/maturin artifact-placement gap (callisto-matrix.md §6.2, and optionally §7's build-action) as its own scoped spec. It requires no new type-system surface — PlatformTargetKind/PlatformTarget/artifact_name_for_triple already model this case correctly (findings 1-4) — and it is the most validated, most demanded piece (finding 11 shows it was fully designed once; finding 22 shows napi-rs's own ecosystem convention already assumes this step exists). This should land before any generalization work, both because it's independently valuable and because its 'gh run download → move into package dir → publish' shape is the natural reference pattern a later CLI-binary spec would extend.\n\nTier 2 — extend the existing matrix/PublishTarget model, don't replace it: CLI-binary-per-platform GitHub Release distribution (the cargo-dist problem, Rust-only) fits callisto's demonstrated shape and 'lean on native tools' philosophy (findings 9, 15-17) better than it first appears, because (a) it reuses the same triple-identity/hostRunner/useCross machinery already built for napi/maturin (findings 3-4), needing only a new PlatformTargetKind variant plus a generalized artifact_name_for_triple, and (b) uploading to a GitHub Release can be done via `gh release upload`, a native tool call, consistent with how callisto already shells out to cargo/npm/twine for real execution (finding 8's resolved contradiction: callisto does execute real publishes, it does not merely plan them). This also finally gives GitHubRelease's already-reserved PublishTarget enum slot (currently orphaned by design, finding 7) real execution behavior instead of permanent warn-and-skip (finding 6).\n\nTier 3 — treat as a distinct, later, explicitly-scoped non-goal-for-now: Homebrew tap-formula generation should NOT be folded into the same abstraction as Tiers 1-2. Unlike registry publishes or GitHub Release uploads, no native tool performs formula generation and cross-repo push — `brew` itself doesn't publish formulas; goreleaser and cargo-dist reimplement that templating+push logic themselves (findings 20-21). Building it in callisto means reimplementing that logic, which contradicts the 'coordinate what's already there, don't reimplement' philosophy the maintainer has stated. It also requires writing to a second repository with separate credentials — a capability class nothing else in callisto has — and directly conflicts with ARCHITECTURE.md §13's zero-network-calls framing and §2.2's registry-only scope table (findings 9, 15). This is not evidence that Homebrew support is wrong to build, only that it is architecturally and philosophically a different kind of feature than Tiers 1-2, and needs its own explicit spec with the maintainer's sign-off on relaxing the zero-network-calls/coordinator invariant — not an assumed extension of the matrix type system.\n\nGo binary distribution: leave entirely out of scope. This is not a gap, it's an existing, explicit, twice-stated non-goal (findings 10, 12) with no evidence of demand to revisit it.\n\nA supporting structural observation: the current model's inability to represent CLI-binaries/Homebrew is not one unifying gap but two separate clusters — the build/identity side (PlatformTargetKind, PlatformTarget's triple-only fields, artifact_name_for_triple, the 18-triple table) and the distribution/sink side (PublishTarget's closed variant set). This mirrors goreleaser's own build/archive/release/announce separation (finding 20) — evidence that if/when generalization does happen, decoupling build-target identity from distribution-sink is the right shape to extend toward, but that shape should be earned incrementally through Tiers 1 and 2, not designed wholesale before either ships.",
